  process: questions, approaches, a design you approve section by section, then a written spec. Say the word if you'd
  rather I collapse it to a short in-chat design.

  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the settings module [3mselects[0m an environment at runtime, or just [3mcentralizes[0m the values so a human
  edits one place.

  - [1mRuntime selection[0m — settings holds a map of all environments (dev/staging/prod) and picks one on load, from hostname
    or an override. Buys you: one deployed artifact works everywhere, no edit-before-deploy step. Costs: every
    environment's URLs ship to the browser (fine for endpoints, bad if you ever add a secret), and you need a selection
    rule that's correct on every host you deploy to. Hard to reverse cheaply once other code reads [38;5;153msettings.current[39m.
  - [1mSingle-value centralization[0m — settings exports one flat config object; changing environment means editing that file
    (or a deploy step rewrites it). Buys you: dead simple, nothing leaks, no selection logic to get wrong. Costs:
    switching environments is a code change, so local-vs-prod testing means editing and reverting. Cheap to upgrade to
    runtime selection later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — a build step substitutes values per environment. Buys you: the cleanest production story.
    Costs: introduces a toolchain this repo currently doesn't have at all. Not cheap to reverse.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime selection with hostname detection plus a manual override.[0m Your stated reason for the change
  is "easier to change environments," and centralization alone doesn't actually make switching easier — it just
  relocates the edit. Build-time injection genuinely solves it but drags in a bundler, which is a big step for a repo
  with zero dependencies.
